Mathematically, the Sachs-Wolfe effect (a primary contributor to CMB anisotropy) relates temperature fluctuations to potential gradients. If there were a large, super-horizon inhomogeneity (e.g., a massive density difference) beyond our observable universe, it would imprint a significant, misaligned intrinsic dipole on the CMB, even after accounting for our local motion. The fact that we do not observe such a residual dipole, beyond what is consistent with noise, strongly implies that the universe is homogeneous and isotropic on scales extending at least one horizon length beyond what we can directly observe.

